summary.value:
This paper proposes a Riemannian Fuzzy K-means (RFK) algorithm to cluster data on product manifolds. Classical heuristic K-means clustering methods (Euclidean or Riemannian) alternate between the centroid and membership estimation. The proposed RFK extends the indicator membership to differentiable fuzzy membership and reduces the clustering problem to a single iteration on solving the Riemannian centroids by a gradient algorithm. This reduces the computational cost without computing the Frechet means. Some synthetic Gaussian mixture, graph- and VAE-encoded examples are used to demonstrate the computational gain and loss reduction.

The reviewers raised several major weakness and questions:

- lack of justification to assume multiple manifold structures for the same data;
- lack of clarity, e.g., in how embedding is performed in hyperbolic space;
- limited comparison to Riemannian baselines.
